Create a pod =>  Kubectl run nginx --image=nginx
Create a  pod via yaml => kubectl create -f pod.defination.yaml
Create a yaml from declerative => kubectl run redis --image=redis123 --dry-run=client -o yaml >tt.yaml
Edit a pod => kubectl edit pod redis
Get properties of yaml => kubectl explain replicaset
Scale a replica set => k scale rs new-replica-set --replicas=2 or k edit rs new-replica-set
Create a deployment and output it into yaml =>  kubectl create deployment --image=nginx nginx --replicas=4 --dry-run=client -o yaml > nginx-deployment.yaml
Get pods from a specific namespace => k get pods -n research
Create a pod in a specific namespace => k run redis --image=redis -n finance
List pods from all namespace => k get pods -A
Create a Service named redis-service of type ClusterIP to expose pod redis on port 6379   => kubectl expose pod redis --port=6379 --target-port=6379 --name=redis-service
(if we dont specifict target port then it will be same as the port mentioned in command)
Create a Service named nginx of type NodePort to expose pod nginx's port 80 on port 30080 on the nodes: kubectl expose pod nginx --type=NodePort --port=80 --name=nginx-service --dry-run=client -o yaml
Replace a pod => k replace --force -f nginx.yaml
If want to schedule a pod on a specific node, add this in pod.yaml => nodeName: controlplane
Get pods with env:dev labels => k get pods --selector env=dev
For checking multiple labels => k get pods --selector env=prod,bu=finance,tier=frontend
Create a taint on node01 with key of spray, value of mortein and effect of NoSchedule => k taint node node01 spray=mortein:NoSchedule
Untaint => k taint node node01 spray=mortein:NoSchedule-
Apply a label color=blue to node node01 => k label nodes node01 color=blue --overwrite=true (PLease see how to apply nodeaffinity in pods yaml)
Edit an existing pod => kubectl get pod webapp -o yaml > my-new-pod.yaml (OR) kubectl edit pod <pod name>
Request (for scheduling fairness) & Limit (for protection/safety) => Cpu should have atleast request | Memory should definately have limit (because when pod uses excessive memory pod crashes due to OOM)  | LimitRange is a Kubernetes object that sets default/min/max resource constraints for Pods or Containers within a namespace.
A DaemonSet ensures that a copy of a Pod runs on every node (or selected nodes) in the Kubernetes cluster. If you want to create a ds via CLI first create a deployment -> change kind to "daemonset" and remove replicas as ds does not have replica.
Static Pods are pods started directly by kubelet from local files, not by the Kubernetes control plane. Static pod file path is usually configured in /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml .
Prioirty class : -1 billion to +2 billion range . Higher the prioirty more important is the pod . => k get prioirtyclass Preemption Policy decides whether a high-priority pod is allowed to remove lower-priority pods to get resources. Two possible values :- Preemptlowerprioirity or Never
While working with custom scheduler , how to find which scheduler scheduled the pod => kubectl get events -o wide and you can also investigate logs of that custom scheduler via => kubectl logs my-custom-scheduler
View enabled admission controller => kube-apiserver -h | grep enable-admission-plugins or in case of kubectl exec -it kube-apiserver-controlplane -- kube-apiserver -h |grep enable-admission-plugin
To enable a new admission controller (Two types -> Mutating, validation) , go inside /etc/kubernetes/maniefests/kube-apiserver.yaml (there you would find a section of enableadmissionplugins) . If you want to disable a default admission controller => in the same file add another line with disenableadmissionplugins
To enable HPA autoscaling/monitoring - first prereq is to install metric server.
Check logs for a pod ==> k logs -f podname containername
Rolling Update :- If you dont specify a update strategy while creating the deployment - it will be by default rolling update . In Rolling update - we take down older version pods and bring up newer version pods one by one.
In Docker, CMD is a Dockerfile instruction that specifies the default command to run when a container starts. The command field corresponds to ENTRYPOINT, and the args field corresponds to CMD in some container runtimes.
Secrets and config maps can be injected into pods in three ways -> 1 specific vars/secrets 2 entire vars/secret 3 mount as a volume
Encrypting secret data at rest => Encrypting data at rest in Kubernetes means encrypting sensitive data (such as Secrets) before it is stored in the etcd database, so it cannot be read directly from disk or backups.It is configured on the kube-apiserver using an EncryptionConfiguration file and protects data stored in the cluster. https://kubernetes.io/docs/tasks/administer-cluster/encrypt-data/#encrypting-your-data
Types of Multi-container pods -> 1 INIT CONTAINER=> first init container starts and ends then main pod runs 2 SIDE CAR CONTAINER=> its just like INIT where sidecar starts first but instead of ending it runs throughout the lifecycle of pod 3 COLOCATED CONTAINERS => both containers run throughout the lifecycle of pod 
Exec into pod ==> k exec -it app -- /bin/bash   OR k exec -it app -- /bin/sh
Kubernetes Upgrade || k drain nodename (this will drain node as well as mark it unschedulable) (K cordon nodename) || To remove it ==> k uncordon nodename
Kube api server => version x | controller manager, scheduler, kubectl => x-1 | kubelet , kube-proxy x-2 | And we should only jump 1 level version for ex -> 1.9 to 1.10
To find out OS of node => cat /etc/*release*
Bckup of all resources => k get all --all-namespace -o yaml > all-deploy-services.yaml

Networking commands
ip addr , ip link , ip route

